gm-method/rendering (a self-introduced regression: a guard added in onBeforeRender that queries the BOUND GL buffer's size to skip 'over-buffer' draw ranges SILENTLY DROPS valid geometry). An instanced/cluster mesh that takes over drawing via onBeforeRender runs BEFORE three binds THAT mesh's index/vertex buffers (three binds per-draw, after onBeforeRender). So gl.getBufferParameter(ELEMENT_ARRAY_BUFFER, BUFFER_SIZE) inside onBeforeRender returns the PREVIOUSLY-bound (usually smaller) buffer's size, not this mesh's -> a 'skip ranges exceeding the bound buffer' guard wrongly skips most in-view draw ranges every frame -> the model renders as disconnected fragments ('only parts'). DECISIVE WITNESS: instrument the draw to count clusters-passing-frustum vs clusters-actually-drawn -- a large gap (e.g. 177 drawn of 414 visible) localizes it to the draw-submission guard, not the cull or the geometry; a force-draw-all-clusters A/B screenshot that reveals MORE connected structure than the normal render proves valid geometry is being hidden. LESSON 1: do NOT query bound-buffer state in onBeforeRender -- the binding is stale; the correct CPU-side size is geometry.index.array.byteLength (always full + correct there). LESSON 2: a guard meant to suppress a TRANSIENT cold-load condition (GPU upload lag) must not also fire in steady state -- if you cannot distinguish the two from the hook (GPU upload completion is not queryable), do NOT guard the draw at all; fix the actual victim instead (here: harden the OTHER subsystem whose init the transient error-storm was poisoning, leaving the draw path untouched). When a prior fix is later found to cause a NEW symptom, REVERT it first and re-witness, rather than layering a second guard.